      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Doole sits at the junction of Farm Roads 503 and 765 in northwestern McCulloch County, about 12 miles north of U.S. Highway 87.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doole%2C_Texas",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Early Doole economy centered on ranching, with the Gansel family opening a crossroads school in the early 1900s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/doole-texas-ghost-town/",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A 1950s drought forced many farmers to abandon operations, accelerating Doole’s decline.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/doole-texas-ghost-town/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Today, a handful of families live amid weathered ruins, including an intact blacksmith shop and old baseball field.",
